.TH "KMSTATUS" "1" "2026-10-02" "0.1" "DOCUMENTATION"
.SH NAME
kmstatus \- dynamic status bar
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]kmstatus\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-config\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-\-socketpath\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-xwindow\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]kmstatus\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-socketpath\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]]
\f[B]\-\-refresh\f[R] | \f[B]\-\-text\f[R] \f[I]text\f[R] |
\f[B]\-\-text\-unset\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]kmstatus\f[R] \f[B]\-\-print\-template\f[R] | \f[B]\-\-doc\f[R] |
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R] | \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
kmstatus displays system information as a single line of text.
It prints the line to the standard output, or sets it as the name of the
X root window, which is where dwm reads its status text from.
.PP
The line consists of segments, each representing a specific hardware or
software component (CPU, memory, network, audio, etc.).
Each segment has a parser, which periodically reads data from system
files or external programs, and a template, which formats the data.
Segments, their order, refresh intervals and templates are set in the
configuration file.
.PP
The running instance (the main process) can be controlled by running
kmstatus again with a control command, e.g.
to refresh segments immediately or to display a custom text.
Control commands are sent to the main process through a unix socket.
.PP
See CONFIGURATION, SEGMENTS and TEMPLATES sections for details.
See EXAMPLES section for a dwm setup.
.SH QUICK REFERENCE
The following command line options are provided by kmstatus:
.IP
.EX
\-c, \-\-config PATH       path to the configuration file
\-s, \-\-socketpath PATH   path to the socket (default
                        /tmp/kmstatus.sock)
\-x, \-\-xwindow           set the status as the X root window name
    \-\-print\-template    print a configuration template
    \-\-doc               print this documentation
\-v, \-\-version           print the version
\-h, \-\-help              print a short help
.EE
.PP
The following control commands are provided by kmstatus:
.IP
.EX
\-r, \-\-refresh           refresh segments and the status now
\-t, \-\-text TEXT         display a custom text (empty clears it)
\-u, \-\-text\-unset        clear the custom text
.EE
.PP
The following options can be set for each segment in the configuration
file:
.IP
.EX
parsername         string     (required)
refreshinterval    duration   (default \(dq1s\(dq)
template           string
.EE
.PP
The following parsers are available as segments:
.IP
.EX
processes          icons of running programs
bluetooth          bluetooth service, controller and device state
audio              speaker and microphone volume
cpu                CPU load and frequency
temperature        temperature sensors
mem                memory and swap usage
network            network interfaces\(aq transfer
clock              date and time
.EE
.PP
The following template functions are provided by segments:
.IP
.EX
bar                cpu        load as a block character
human              mem        human\-readable size (from kB)
human              network    human\-readable size (from bytes)
ljust              network    pad a value with spaces to a width
hasPrefix          network    test if a string starts with a prefix
format             clock      format the time with a layout
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
Options can be given with one or two dashes, e.g.
\f[B]\-c\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-config\f[R] are the same.
.SS \-c, \-\-config \f[I]path\f[R]
Path to the configuration file.
If not given, kmstatus looks up the following paths and uses the first
existing file:
.IP
.EX
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kmstatus/kmstatusrc.toml
$HOME/.config/kmstatus/kmstatusrc.toml
.EE
.PP
If none exists, the built\-in default configuration is used.
A given path to a file which does not exist is an error.
.SS \-s, \-\-socketpath \f[I]path\f[R] (default /tmp/kmstatus.sock)
Path to the unix socket used for control commands.
The main process creates the socket and control commands connect to it,
so both need to be given the same path.
Running several instances requires a separate socket for each of them.
.SS \-x, \-\-xwindow
Set the status as the name (WM_NAME) of the X root window instead of
printing it.
This is the way of setting the status text of dwm.
Requires kmstatus to be built with X support (\f[B]make buildx\f[R]).
.SS \-\-print\-template
Print a configuration template and exit.
The template is the default configuration, used when no configuration
file is found.
It is a starting point for creating a configuration file (see
CONFIGURATION).
.SS \-\-doc
Print this documentation and exit.
.SS \-v, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SS \-h, \-\-help
Print a short help and exit.
.SH CONTROL COMMANDS
A control command sends a message to the running main process and exits.
It fails if the main process is not running.
Only one control command can be given at a time.
.SS \-r, \-\-refresh
Parse data of all segments immediately and render the status.
This is useful when a change should be visible right away instead of
after the segment\(aqs refresh interval, e.g.
after changing the volume.
The cpu and network segments are not refreshed, since their values
(load, transfer speed) are calculated between parses in equal time
intervals.
.SS \-t, \-\-text \f[I]text\f[R]
Display a custom text at the beginning of the status, before all
segments.
The text is surrounded with a space on each side.
An empty text clears the text.
.SS \-u, \-\-text\-unset
Clear the custom text.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is written in TOML.
It consists of a list of segments, each defined in a
\f[B][[segment]]\f[R] table.
Segments are displayed in the order of definition.
The same parser can be used by more than one segment, e.g.
to display it with different templates.
.IP
.EX
[[segment]]
parsername = \(dqcpu\(dq
refreshinterval = \(dq1s\(dq
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq{{ .Load | printf \(dq%4.1f\(dq }}%\(dq\(dq\(dq

[[segment]]
parsername = \(dqclock\(dq
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq {{ . | format \(dq15:04\(dq }}\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
An unknown option (e.g.
a misspelled one) is an error.
.PP
When no configuration file is found, the built\-in default configuration
is used.
It is printed by \f[B]\-\-print\-template\f[R] and can be viewed online:
.IP
.EX
https://github.com/maicher/kmstatus/blob/master/internal/config/kmstatusrc.example.toml
.EE
.SS Creating a configuration file
The easiest way is to start from the configuration template and adjust
it:
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p \(ti/.config/kmstatus
kmstatus \-\-print\-template > \(ti/.config/kmstatus/kmstatusrc.toml
.EE
.PP
Edit the file and test it in a terminal, without affecting the running
status bar, by using a separate socket:
.IP
.EX
cd \(ti/.config/kmstatus
kmstatus \-c kmstatusrc.toml \-s /tmp/kmstatus\-test.sock
.EE
.PP
The configuration is read on start, so restart the running kmstatus to
apply it.
.SS parsername (string)
Name of the parser, one of the parsers listed in the SEGMENTS section.
.SS refreshinterval (duration) (default \(dq1s\(dq)
How often the segment parses its data.
A duration is a number with a unit, e.g.
\(dq500ms\(dq, \(dq5s\(dq, \(dq1m\(dq, \(dq1m30s\(dq.
.PP
The status is rendered with the shortest refresh interval of all
segments, but at least once a minute.
For example, a configuration of segments refreshed every \(dq1s\(dq and
\(dq1m\(dq renders the status every second, but the data of the latter
segment changes once a minute.
.SS template (string)
Template of the segment\(aqs output.
See the TEMPLATES section for the syntax and the SEGMENTS section for
the data available in each segment.
The processes segment uses its own format, see its description.
.SH TEMPLATES
Templates use the syntax of the Go text/template package.
Values are inserted with \f[B]{{ }}\f[R] actions, everything else is
printed as it is:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dqCPU: {{ .Load }}%\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Newline characters are removed from templates.
Thanks to this, a long template can be split into lines in a TOML
multi\-line string (\f[B]\(dq\(dq\(dq\f[R]), but a space must be written
explicitly where it is needed:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
 {{ if .OutMuted }}muted{{ else }}{{ .OutVolume }}%{{ end }}
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Data
A field or a method of the segment\(aqs data is accessed with a dot and
its name, e.g.
\f[B].Load\f[R].
A dot alone is the whole data, e.g.
the time in the clock segment.
.SS Pipelines
A value can be passed to a function with a pipe \f[B]|\f[R].
The passed value becomes the last argument of the function:
.IP
.EX
{{ .Load | printf \(dq%4.1f\(dq }}     is    {{ printf \(dq%4.1f\(dq .Load }}
{{ .Name | hasPrefix \(dqen\(dq }}     is    {{ hasPrefix \(dqen\(dq .Name }}
.EE
.PP
Hence, for a comparison it is clearer to write the arguments in order:
.IP
.EX
{{ if gt .SwapUsed 0 }}          true if .SwapUsed > 0
{{ if .SwapUsed | gt 0 }}        true if 0 > .SwapUsed (!)
.EE
.SS Built\-in functions
The following functions are available in all templates:
.IP
.EX
and, or, not              boolean operators
eq, ne, lt, le, gt, ge    comparisons (==, !=, <, <=, >, >=)
printf                    format values, e.g. printf \(dq%.1f\(dq
len, index, slice         length, element, part of a value
.EE
.SS Conditions
.IP
.EX
{{ if .IsServiceActive }}on{{ else }}off{{ end }}
{{ if or (eq .Name \(dqk10temp\(dq) (eq .Name \(dqamdgpu\(dq) }}
{{ .Value }}
{{ end }}
.EE
.SH SEGMENTS
.SS processes
Displays an icon for each running program from a list.
.PP
The template of this segment is not a Go template.
It is a list of lines, each consisting of an icon and a process name
separated by whitespace.
The icon can not contain whitespace, the process name may contain
spaces.
An icon is displayed when the name of any running process starts with
the given process name.
Icons are displayed in the order of lines without separators, so a
separator should be a part of the icon.
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
F firefox
V vpn
S Socket Process
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Process names are read with \f[B]ps \-e \-o comm=\f[R].
The kernel truncates them to 15 characters.
.SS bluetooth
Displays the state of the bluetooth service, controller and the
connected device.
.IP
.EX
\&.IsServiceActive      bool    bluetooth service is running
\&.IsControllerPowered  bool    bluetooth controller is on
\&.DeviceType           string  type of the connected device, e.g.
                              \(dqaudio\-headset\(dq, \(dqaudio\-card\(dq, or
                              \(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Requires \f[B]systemctl\f[R] and \f[B]bluetoothctl\f[R].
.SS audio
Displays the volume of the default speaker (sink) and of the microphone.
The microphone is the audio source with \(dqMicrophone\(dq in its name
or description (see \f[B]pamixer \-\-list\-sources\f[R]).
It is not necessarily the default source.
.IP
.EX
\&.OutAvailable         bool    speaker state could be read
\&.OutMuted             bool    speaker is muted
\&.OutVolume            int     speaker volume in percents
\&.InAvailable          bool    microphone was found
\&.InMuted              bool    microphone is muted
\&.InVolume             int     microphone volume in percents
.EE
.PP
Requires \f[B]pamixer\f[R].
Since the volume usually changes from a keybinding, a long refresh
interval combined with the \f[B]\-\-refresh\f[R] control command is
recommended (see EXAMPLES).
.SS cpu
Displays the CPU load and frequency, averaged over all cores.
.IP
.EX
\&.Load                 float   load in percents since the
                              previous parse
\&.Freq                 int     frequency in kHz
\&.FreqMHz              float   frequency in MHz
\&.FreqGHz              float   frequency in GHz

bar LOAD                      load as a block character:
                              \(dq \(dq below 2%, \(dq░\(dq below 9%,
                              \(dq▒\(dq below 30%, \(dq▓\(dq below 90%,
                              \(dq█\(dq otherwise
.EE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
{{ .FreqGHz | printf \(dq%3.1f\(dq }}GHz {{ .Load | bar }}
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Data is read from \f[B]/proc/stat\f[R] and
\f[B]/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq\f[R].
Not affected by the \f[B]\-\-refresh\f[R] control command.
.SS temperature
Displays temperature sensors.
The template is executed once for each sensor.
.IP
.EX
\&.Name                 string  name of the sensor
\&.Value                int     temperature in Celsius
\&.Celsius              int     temperature in Celsius
\&.Fahrenheit           int     temperature in Fahrenheit
.EE
.PP
Example, to display a single sensor:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
{{ if eq .Name \(dqk10temp\(dq }} {{ .Celsius }}°C{{ end }}
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Sensors are read from thermal zones
(\f[B]/sys/devices/virtual/thermal/thermal_zone*/temp\f[R]), named after
their type, e.g.
\(dqx86_pkg_temp\(dq, \(dqacpitz\(dq.
If there are no thermal zones, hwmon sensors are read
(\f[B]/sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/temp1_input\f[R]), named after their
driver, e.g.
\(dqk10temp\(dq, \(dqamdgpu\(dq, \(dqnvme\(dq.
To list the names, use the template:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq{{ .Name }} \(dq
.EE
.SS mem
Displays memory and swap usage.
Sizes are in kB.
.IP
.EX
\&.Total                int     total memory
\&.Used                 int     used memory (without buffers and
                              cache)
\&.UsedPercentage       float   used memory in percents
\&.SwapTotal            int     total swap
\&.SwapUsed             int     used swap
\&.SwapUsedPercentage   float   used swap in percents

human PRECISION SIZE          size in kB with a unit (M, G, T)
                              and PRECISION decimal places, e.g.
                              1.5G; sizes below 1M are printed
                              as a number
.EE
.PP
Example, to display swap only when used:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
 {{ .Used | human 1 }}/{{ .Total | human 0 }}
{{ if gt .SwapUsed 0 }} swap {{ .SwapUsed | human 1 }}{{ end }}
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Data is read from \f[B]/proc/meminfo\f[R].
.SS network
Displays transfer of network interfaces.
The template is executed once for each interface, including the loopback
(lo).
.IP
.EX
\&.Name                 string  name of the interface, e.g. \(dqeno1\(dq
\&.Rx                   int     received bytes per second
\&.Tx                   int     transmitted bytes per second
\&.RxTotal              int     received bytes in total
\&.TxTotal              int     transmitted bytes in total

human PRECISION SIZE          size in bytes with a unit (k, M,
                              G, T) and PRECISION decimal
                              places, e.g. 2k; sizes below 1k
                              are printed as a number
ljust WIDTH VALUE             VALUE (a string or an int)
                              preceded by spaces to WIDTH
                              characters, for a constant width
hasPrefix PREFIX STRING       STRING starts with PREFIX
.EE
.PP
Example, to display wired interfaces only:
.IP
.EX
template = \(dq\(dq\(dq
{{ if .Name | hasPrefix \(dqen\(dq }}
 {{ .Name }}
 {{ .Rx | human 0 | ljust 4 }}
 {{ .Tx | human 0 | ljust 4 }}
{{ end }}
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Speeds are averaged since the previous parse.
Data is read from \f[B]/proc/net/dev\f[R].
Not affected by the \f[B]\-\-refresh\f[R] control command.
.SS clock
Displays the current date and time.
The data (dot) is the current time, read on each render.
.IP
.EX
format LAYOUT TIME            TIME formatted according to LAYOUT
.EE
.PP
The layout is the reference time \f[B]Mon Jan 2 15:04:05 MST 2006\f[R]
written in the desired format:
.IP
.EX
{{ . | format \(dq2006\-01\-02 15:04:05\(dq }}      2024\-05\-01 08:10:19
{{ . | format \(dqMon 02 Jan 15:04\(dq }}         Wed 01 May 08:10
.EE
.PP
Methods of the time can be used as well, e.g.
\f[B]{{ .Weekday }}\f[R], \f[B]{{ .Hour }}\f[R].
.PP
The refresh interval of the clock only affects how often the status is
rendered.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start kmstatus as the status bar of dwm, e.g.
in \f[B]\(ti/.xinitrc\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
kmstatus \-x &
exec dwm
.EE
.PP
Change the volume and update the status immediately, e.g.
in a dwm keybinding:
.IP
.EX
pamixer \-i 5 && kmstatus \-r
.EE
.PP
In dwm\(aqs config.h (with \f[B]#include <X11/XF86keysym.h>\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
static const char *volup[] = {
    \(dqsh\(dq, \(dq\-c\(dq, \(dqpamixer \-i 5 && kmstatus \-r\(dq, NULL
};
{ 0, XF86XK_AudioRaiseVolume, spawn, {.v = volup } },
.EE
.PP
Display a notice while a long task runs:
.IP
.EX
kmstatus \-t \(dqbackup\(dq && rsync \-a \(ti/ /mnt/backup/; kmstatus \-u
.EE
.PP
Run a second instance in a terminal, with another configuration and
socket:
.IP
.EX
kmstatus \-c \(ti/.config/kmstatus/term.toml \-s /tmp/kmstatus\-term.sock
.EE
.PP
Test a configuration, without affecting the running status bar:
.IP
.EX
kmstatus \-c ./kmstatusrc.toml \-s /tmp/kmstatus\-test.sock
.EE
.SH FILES
.IP
.EX
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kmstatus/kmstatusrc.toml     configuration file
$HOME/.config/kmstatus/kmstatusrc.toml        configuration file
/tmp/kmstatus.sock                            default socket
.EE
.PP
The default configuration is built into kmstatus and printed by
\f[B]\-\-print\-template\f[R].
Its source is in the repository:
.IP
.EX
https://github.com/maicher/kmstatus/blob/master/internal/config/kmstatusrc.example.toml
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.SS XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory of the configuration file, see \f[B]\-\-config\f[R].
.SS HOME
Directory of the configuration file, if not found in XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
.SS DISPLAY
X display to set the status on, with \f[B]\-\-xwindow\f[R].
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The main process exits with status 1 on an error, e.g.
an invalid configuration, or when another instance already uses the
socket.
A socket left by an instance that did not exit cleanly is removed on
start.
.PP
Errors which occur while running, e.g.
a sensor that can not be read, are printed to the standard error and do
not stop kmstatus.
An external program (pamixer, bluetoothctl, ps) is stopped if it does
not finish in 2 seconds.
.PP
A control command exits with status 1 if the main process is not
running, and with status 2 on invalid options.
.SH BUGS
kmstatus runs on Linux only.
It was tested on Arch Linux and Ubuntu, with Intel and AMD CPUs.
.PP
Report bugs at https://github.com/maicher/kmstatus/issues.
